      "text": [
        {
          "id": "title",
          "visible": true,
          "kicker": "#2 · chapter V, PDF pp.118–148 (hail at pp.120–122)",
          "title": "The body returns from a gaze that precedes it",
          "italic": "The scene demonstrates a socially prepared image reorganising lived bodily possibility — not history become biology, but the occasion through which the image arrives.",
          "body": "",
          "x": 0.04,
          "y": 0.05,
          "width": 560,
          "size": 30,
          "align": "left"
        },
        {
          "id": "body1",
          "visible": true,
          "kicker": "",
          "title": "",
          "italic": "",
          "body": "A person moves through a public world with a practical bodily orientation: the relation between body and surroundings makes movement possible before the body must be considered as an object of racial classification. The racial hail interrupts. In the train scene the repeated hail moves from a smile toward the child's fear; Fanon is made responsible for body, race and ancestors, and the space left around him becomes part of the event. The body-schema gives way to the epidermal racial schema. The two schemata stay",
          "x": 0.04,
          "y": 0.47,
          "width": 620,
          "size": 14,
          "align": "left",
          "w": 520
        },
        {
          "id": "body2",
          "visible": true,
          "kicker": "",
          "title": "",
          "italic": "",
          "body": "distinguishable: the historico-racial schema carries the inherited field through which the body is interpreted; the epidermal schema gives that field its force at the visible body — not history become biology, but the racialised appearance as occasion through which a socially prepared image reorganises lived bodily possibility. Chapter V continues beyond the hail through attempted answers and their frustration; its ending supplies no completed reconciliation, preventing the scene from becoming an illustration solved by naming its schema.\n\nOccurrences: the train and the child's fear (#2); the two schemata (#2); the frustrated answers and open ending (#2, #4).",
          "x": 0.6,
          "y": 0.47,
          "width": 505,
          "size": 14,
          "align": "left"
        }
      ],
