      "text": [
        {
          "id": "title",
          "visible": true,
          "kicker": "#4 · chapter VII, PDF pp.207–220; conclusion, pp.221–229",
          "title": "Recognition must change the relation that distributes it",
          "italic": "The whole demonstrates the destination: not private self-esteem but the social relation becoming revisable — a transformation of recognition makes its terms answerable to the person they concern.",
          "body": "",
          "x": 0.04,
          "y": 0.05,
          "width": 560,
          "size": 30,
          "align": "left"
        },
        {
          "id": "body1",
          "visible": true,
          "kicker": "",
          "title": "",
          "italic": "",
          "body": "The language and body scenes make private self-esteem an insufficient destination: a person can improve his self-account while continuing to be received through the same inherited racial measure. Chapter VII distinguishes comparison under a socially governing white standard from individually centred rivalry; recognition is pursued through a relation requiring more than one participant's changed self-description. If access is granted only on condition that the hierarchy remains authoritative,",
          "x": 0.04,
          "y": 0.47,
          "width": 620,
          "size": 14,
          "align": "left",
          "w": 520
        },
        {
          "id": "body2",
          "visible": true,
          "kicker": "",
          "title": "",
          "italic": "",
          "body": "increased access does not change the relation that demanded the mask. The relation is reciprocal as a demand while the historical distribution of power stays unequal: one participant can inherit an authority to classify that the other does not possess in the same way. The conclusion keeps de-alienation answerable to different material situations — the demand cannot be reduced to intellectual self-revision, and the earlier bodily disruption is not forgotten when recognition becomes thinkable.\n\nOccurrences: the demanding reciprocity (#4); the inherited authority to classify (#1, #4); the conclusion's material answerability (#2, #4).",
